vmsotel @VERSION@ for OpenVMS (@ARCH@)
======================================

An OpenTelemetry agent: it reports this system's CPU, memory, paging,
processes, disks and uptime -- and, if asked, chosen processes, the
operator log and the audit journal -- to an OpenTelemetry collector over
OTLP/HTTP, with or without TLS. It connects out to the collector;
nothing listens on this system. It runs as its own account, never as
SYSTEM.

Requires: OpenVMS x86-64 (tested on E9.2-4) or Integrity (tested on
V8.4-2L3), VSI TCP/IP Services, and VSI's SSL3 kit.

Setting up (as SYSTEM)
----------------------
1. Create the agent's account, set the protections, and create the
   configuration, once:

     $ @VMSOTEL$ROOT:[COM]VMSOTEL$CONFIGURE

   It asks for the account (default VMSOTEL) and, for a new account,
   its UIC: choose a group of its own, above MAXSYSGROUP (e.g. [360,1]).
   The account cannot log in by any route; the agent still runs as it.
   Its authorized privileges are NETMBX and TMPMBX, plus WORLD and
   READALL only if VMSOTEL.CONF needs them -- so run it again whenever
   you turn on per-process metrics or a log. It changes only an account
   it created.

2. Edit VMSOTEL$ROOT:[CONF]VMSOTEL.CONF (made from VMSOTEL.CONF_EXAMPLE,
   which explains every setting). Set host to the collector. For TLS,
   put the collector's certificate (or its CA's) in [CONF] and name it:

     tls = yes
     cafile = VMSOTEL$ROOT:[CONF]COLLECTOR.CRT

   The certificate is always verified; there is no setting to skip it.
   A file you add to [CONF] must be readable by the account:

     $ SET FILE/OWNER=VMSOTEL VMSOTEL$ROOT:[CONF]*.*;*

   Check what it would report, without contacting the collector:

     $ VMSOTEL := $VMSOTEL$ROOT:[BIN]VMSOTEL.EXE
     $ VMSOTEL --SELFTEST

3. Start it:

     $ @VMSOTEL$ROOT:[COM]VMSOTEL_START

   and stop it, letting it send what it has queued:

     $ @VMSOTEL$ROOT:[COM]VMSOTEL_STOP

   Both need IMPERSONATE and WORLD (SYSTEM has them).

4. At boot and shutdown, add to SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM (after
   TCP/IP) and SYS$MANAGER:SYSHUTDWN.COM:

     $ @SYS$STARTUP:VMSOTEL$STARTUP.COM
     $ @SYS$STARTUP:VMSOTEL$SHUTDOWN.COM

   VMSOTEL$STARTUP starts the agent if VMSOTEL$CONFIGURE was told to
   (SYS$MANAGER:VMSOTEL$CONFIG.COM).

Files
-----
  VMSOTEL$ROOT:[BIN]   VMSOTEL_TLS.EXE (used when present: it also speaks
                       plain HTTP), VMSOTEL.EXE, VMSOTEL_LAUNCH.EXE (starts
                       the agent as its account)
  VMSOTEL$ROOT:[COM]   VMSOTEL_START, _STOP, _RUN, _PRIVS, VMSOTEL$CONFIGURE
  VMSOTEL$ROOT:[CONF]  VMSOTEL.CONF, VMSOTEL.CONF_EXAMPLE
  VMSOTEL$ROOT:[LOG]   VMSOTEL.LOG (the agent's, one line per event),
                       VMSOTEL_RUN.LOG (DCL's, five versions kept)
  VMSOTEL$ROOT:[DOC]   this file, OPERATIONS.MD (every log line explained),
                       LICENSE.
  SYS$MANAGER:VMSOTEL$CONFIG.COM   the account, and whether to start at boot

PRODUCT REMOVE VMSOTEL stops the agent and deassigns VMSOTEL$ROOT and
VMSOTEL$USER. VMSOTEL.CONF, the logs, the site settings and the account
are kept, so a reinstall carries on where it left off.

When it does not start, VMSOTEL$ROOT:[LOG]VMSOTEL_RUN.LOG says why;
when it does not send, VMSOTEL.LOG does. The source and the full
documentation are at https://github.com/issinoho/vmsotel.

